Subject: KeyMint TA: ask the device whether a key blob's KEK context is outdated

A key blob records the context its key encryption key was derived from (kek_context), and the
TA hands that context back to the device's RetrieveKeyMaterial::root_kek when it opens the
blob. Nothing asked whether that context is still the one new blobs get, so a device that moves
its keys to another root key had no way to have the old blobs wrapped again.

RetrieveKeyMaterial gains kek_context_is_outdated, false by default. When it says a blob's
context is outdated, the TA answers KEY_REQUIRES_UPGRADE for the blob, as for an old patch
level, and upgradeKey wraps it again under the current context and root key. Keystore and vold
upgrade every blob they use on that answer, so no key is lost on the way.

This is the hook system/keymint has from Android 17 (the same code at cfeefc94bf8c, the commit
Android 17 build 16373615 is made from), brought to Android 16. DSA's KeyMint uses it to move
the blobs made under the built-in root key to the root key the DSA host gives.

Applies to system/keymint at 748182e106e68bb3661f733be3383a1b548fa51b (Android 16).
